+# Agent Note: Web conversation split view
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-14-web-conversation-split.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Comparing two conversations requires keeping one session's transcript visible while navigating another. The Web client staged only its selected session, so rendering an ordinary conversation slot a second time would still bind it to the primary session.
+
+## Decision
+
+The sidebar Session menu adds **Split session**. Selecting it calls `SessionRuntime.openInPane()` and `LayoutController.splitSession()`. The layout store retains one transient secondary `SessionId`; `AppFrame` renders the existing `conversation` slot a second time with that id in `RenderOpts.sessionId`.
+
+`SessionRuntime.paneProvideInfo(id)` resolves the secondary session's standard-kit bundle without changing the primary selection. The renderer propagates an addressed `sessionId` through the nested slot tree with `SessionBindingProvider`, so strict session slots, scoped inject factories, stores, input state, and conversation views read the selected secondary session. The Session object opens its history window and refreshes its direct subagent catalog before the pane is shown.
+
+The secondary pane has the same conversation implementation and controls as the primary pane, with an independent scrollport, per-session store, and input machine. It closes from the pane control or when the same session's menu action is chosen again. It is hidden below 920px and is not persisted across reload.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Duplicate the current conversation without an addressed bundle.** Rejected: both panes would bind to the current Session, which cannot compare two conversations and would share view state and input.
+
+**Create a second bespoke transcript component.** Rejected: a parallel renderer would duplicate slots, session state, chat views, tool cards, and future conversation contributions. The existing `conversation` slot supplies the same feature composition in both panes.
+
+**Persist the secondary selection.** Rejected: split layout is browser viewing state. Restoring a second history window on page load adds connection and resource work before the user asks for it.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The Web GUI presents a sidebar, primary conversation page, and selected secondary conversation page in a VS Code-style side-by-side layout. Each pane has its own transcript, active view, composer, and per-session store; primary navigation remains the global selection target.
+
+The renderer gains one addressed-session option for nested `renderSlot` calls. It is a renderer-local binding override, not a second global selection state. Sessions remain object-layer owners of event windows and standard-kit bundles.
+
+Focused layout, workspace-row, runtime, and renderer tests pin secondary selection, opening, binding, and teardown. The Web composition includes only existing client packages, so `pnpm run dev:web` rebuilds their bundles and the active client HMR receiver applies later browser-plugin edits without a page refresh.
